//
// Phase D2 block integration gate: one whole encoder_bert transformer layer.
//
// Five operator launches -- qkv_proj, the fused attention + output projection,
// pre-add addnorm, the staged FFN, pre-add addnorm again -- over four
// separately compiled ELFs, dispatched through KernelCache.run_sequence, at
// seq_len 4096 / hidden 768 / ffn 3072 / 12 heads x head_dim 64, non-causal.
// Compared element-wise against pattern/reference.py, which is iron's golden
// model's STRUCTURE (its load-bearing draw order, its zeros biases, both
// workload variants) computed in FP32 from bf16-rounded inputs rather than in
// iron's bf16 throughout.
//
// Two recipes, not one: the clean layer, then the same layer with one weight
// perturbed, which must be REJECTED. See the negative control's own section
// below the clean checks for what it injects into and why that target was
// measured rather than chosen.
//
// Every operator here already passed at this width in Phase D1, so a failure
// is an INTEGRATION failure: an argument map, a layout transition, an
// external-kernel collision across a multi-operator sequence, or BO reuse under
// the Phase B allocator. The first of those it caught was the third: the FFN's
// up-projection and the o-projection are both the drain GEMM method at
// DIFFERENT tile_n, compile_gemm_mm names its object from the method alone, and
// the ELF that linked second silently got a 96-wide micro-kernel for its
// 128-wide tile -- exactly zero in 32 of every 128 output columns.
//
// WHY THE PER-BOUNDARY STAGES ARE CHECKED HERE AND NOT ONLY IN THE ARTIFACT
// A layer that ends in a LayerNorm absorbs an enormous amount of upstream
// damage. The 25% of the up-projection that came back zero reached the layer
// output as a 54% mismatch, which says only "something is wrong"; the stage
// list said "ffn_up, and everything before it is exact".
//
// The stages are not sufficient either, and pattern/test_reference.py measures
// the other side of that: swapping the device's tanh GeLU for the erf form
// moves the ffn_gelu boundary by 4.7e-4 absolute and the layer output by
// 6.1e-4, so NO tolerance in this example would see it. That oracle is pinned
// by identity in run_reference_tests.lit, which is in this same suite and needs
// no hardware. Ten boundary numbers localize a defect the end-to-end number
// only reports; neither pins a wrong reference.
//
// NPU serialization lives one layer down, not in the recipe below. Device
// access is locked on /tmp/npu.lock, which KernelCache.ensure_loaded holds
// across binary load and which XRTRunner.run_test holds across load and
// dispatch (python/air/backend/xrt_runner.py:292) -- the same inode
// lit.cfg.py's NPU substitution takes, so this interlocks with every other NPU
// job in the repository while the peano compiles still overlap. Taking the
// outer /tmp/mlir-air-npu.lock here instead would DEADLOCK: the gate command
// that invokes this suite already holds that inode, and BSD flock(2) treats a
// second open() of the same file as a foreign lock, so the nested acquire would
// block against its own parent until the timeout expires.
//
// Editing this header: lit scans the WHOLE file for its directives, so naming
// one in prose -- even inside a comment -- registers as a second directive and
// leaves the test UNRESOLVED. Describe them, never spell them out.
//
// REQUIRES: ryzen_ai_npu2, peano
//
// RUN: mkdir -p test_npu2_block_peano
// RUN: cd test_npu2_block_peano
// RUN: make -f %S/Makefile clean
// RUN: make -f %S/Makefile check-block PEANO_INSTALL_DIR=%PEANO_INSTALL_DIR | FileCheck %s
//
// The configuration first. None of it is a choice: baseline_768 is the only
// family whose GEMMs resolve, and seq 4096 is the only point on the ladder
// where the FFN builds at hidden 768 (its two projections take a different
// tile_n at every point, and only here does the registry put them on different
// METHODS, which is what lets them co-link). A registry change that moved
// either fails here with its cause on screen.
// CHECK: block 4096x768 ffn 3072 12h x 64 (encoder_bert, non-causal)
// CHECK: qkv_proj  fused-cast (registry), ffn up drain / down fused-cast (registry), o_proj drain (registry)
//
// The row-blocking, because it is the one structural concession in the layer
// and it is easy to regress silently. addnorm drives three L3->L1 streams per
// tile against a column's two shim MM2S channels, so it takes exactly one
// kernel call per tile and L1 caps it at 104 rows at this width; the layer's
// 4096 rows go through it as 64 dispatches of the 64x768 pre-add shape D1
// measured. A change that dispatched a WIDER addnorm than was ever validated
// would show up right here.
// CHECK: addnorm pre-add 64x768 x64 dispatches (L1 cap 104)
//
// Every boundary, in execution order, each with zero mismatching elements
// against the golden model at rtol 1.6e-2 and that boundary's own measured
// atol. Named individually rather than counted, so a stage list that dropped an
// entry or repeated one fails here.
// CHECK: [stage] q {{.*}} mismatch       0
// CHECK: [stage] k {{.*}} mismatch       0
// CHECK: [stage] v {{.*}} mismatch       0
// CHECK: [stage] attn_context {{.*}} mismatch       0
// CHECK: [stage] attn_out {{.*}} mismatch       0
// CHECK: [stage] hidden {{.*}} mismatch       0
// CHECK: [stage] ffn_up {{.*}} mismatch       0
// CHECK: [stage] ffn_gelu {{.*}} mismatch       0
// CHECK: [stage] ffn_out {{.*}} mismatch       0
// CHECK: [stage] output {{.*}} mismatch       0
// CHECK: [block] stages: 10/10 clean
//
// Then the layer output itself over all 4096 x 768 elements. Both lines, not
// just the verdict: the first is the comparison's own, the second names the
// operator and shape, so a test wired to the wrong one fails here rather than
// passing quietly.
// CHECK: PASS!
// CHECK: [opcheck] block [4096x768_encoder_bert]: PASS
//
// THE NEGATIVE CONTROL, AND WHY IT IS IN THIS FILE
// Everything above is a positive result, and a positive result from a check
// that cannot fail is worth nothing. The recipe below runs the same layer again
// with one element of ln1_weight perturbed AFTER the golden model was computed
// from the clean value, and requires the comparison to reject it. A block check
// that ignored the injection flag, a reference compared against itself, and a
// tolerance wide enough to swallow a perturbed weight all still report PASS
// above; all three fail here. opcheck.py's expect-failure mode reports the
// control's own verdict rather than inverting an exit status, so a missing
// toolchain, a kernel that fails to link, or an NPU that never came up cannot
// print a passing control -- none of those compared anything.
//
// It shares this file, and therefore this working directory, because the four
// ELFs are cached under it by FINGERPRINT over the built MLIR, the resolved
// registry specs, the device kernel sources and the backend kwargs
// (builders/block_cache.py). Compilation depends on the shape and not on the
// data, so the injected run reuses all four and costs one dispatch instead of a
// second compile of the whole layer. A separate test file would get a separate
// working directory and pay four ELFs to perturb one weight.
//
// WHY ln1_weight AND NOT AN ATTENTION WEIGHT
// Measured, not assumed. At the golden model's val_range 0.05 the attention
// scores land around 5e-3, so the softmax is nearly uniform and attn_out comes
// out around 1e-3 against a residual x around 5e-2 -- the attention half
// contributes a few percent of what the first LayerNorm sees. Perturbing w_o or
// the fused QKV weight therefore moves the output by 3.1e-2 and puts ZERO
// elements outside the band: a control injected there would PASS under
// injection and prove nothing. ln1_weight scales one column of `hidden`, which
// is BOTH the FFN's input and the second addnorm's residual, so it reaches 9%
// of the output through two independent paths. The full table over every
// candidate input is in the block section of opcheck_specs.py.
//
// RUN: make -f %S/Makefile check-block-fault PEANO_INSTALL_DIR=%PEANO_INSTALL_DIR | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=FAULT
//
// The injected element first, so an injection that silently perturbed nothing
// is visible here rather than being read backwards from the verdict. Index 3 is
// ln1_weight in BLOCK_INPUT_NAMES, and it is a vector, hence the one-element
// position.
// FAULT: [fault-inject] input 3 at (0,)
//
// Then the stage list under injection, which is the part an end-to-end verdict
// cannot give. Nothing upstream of the first addnorm reads ln1_weight, so q, k,
// v, attn_context and attn_out must still be exact while `hidden` and
// everything after it must move -- and that split is what says the stage
// comparisons are reading THIS run's device buffers rather than replaying the
// clean run's. A stage list computed from the reference, or from stale pool
// memory, comes back 10/10 clean right here.
// FAULT: [stage] q {{.*}} mismatch       0
// FAULT: [stage] attn_out {{.*}} mismatch       0
// FAULT: [stage] hidden {{.*}} mismatch {{[1-9][0-9]*}}
// FAULT: [block] stages: 5/10 clean
//
// And the verdict in the three forms opcheck.py reports it: the comparison's
// own, the control's reading of it, and the exit-status summary. The middle
// line is the one that distinguishes "the run failed" from "the comparison
// rejected the run", and the last is what the recipe's status comes from.
// FAULT: [opcheck] block [4096x768_encoder_bert]: FAIL
// FAULT: [opcheck] block [4096x768_encoder_bert]: fault-inject input -> FAIL (as required)
// FAULT: NEGATIVE CONTROL: PASS
